ll two to  four times too slow to support real-time  variant use cases like cross filter  so to summarize i led a large  interdisciplinary effort to build the  first dbms performance benchmark to  support real-time interactive  exploration by crossfilter interfaces so  our study shows that when given the  opportunity people will take advantage  of the real-time  uh properties of crossover interfaces  but  in making a benchmark out of the  interaction data we actually found that  existing systems just aren't fast enough  to provide satisfaction performance for  cross-filter even for modest asses sizes  of one two hundred million rows  so now i'll briefly discuss my broader  research agenda and highlight some  ongoing projects that i'm particularly  excited about and share my larger vision  for this work going forward so to  reiterate the question driving my work  is how can we design adaptive systems to  amplify the strengths and mitigate the  weaknesses of system and human analysts  to address this question i conduct  research along these three axes which  also guide my research  agenda for example to further  characterize exploration i plan to  formalize how existing performance  models relate to  each other from models of human  reasoning to models of interface  structure to database cost models  to further optimize exploration i plan  to design more intelligent and  interoperable systems  and to enable broader benchmarking of  exploration systems i plan to leverage  my work and user modeling to do  dynamically derive  benchmarks directly from experiment data  so as part of this research done though  my students and i are interested in  helping people build their own high  performance dashboards for the first  where the first step is to help people  build their own visualization interfaces  especially with languages that are  powerful and expressive like d3  the drawback  a drawback though is that d3 can be  complicated and difficult to learn as a  steep learning 